I also practiced fetching **Top Headlines** from a News API and displaying the news using different React components.

---

## 1. Class-Based Component

A Class-Based Component is a React component created using a JavaScript class.

### Example:


import React, { Component } from "react";

export default class App extends Component {
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h1>My News App</h1>
      </div>
    );
  }
}


### Easy Explanation
In a class component:

* `Component` comes from React.
* `extends Component` means our class becomes a React component.
* `render()` contains the JSX that we want to display.



# 2. Props

**Props = Properties**

Props are used to **send data from one component to another component**.

Usually, data is passed from a **parent component to a child component**.

For example:


<NewsItem
  title="Pakistan wins the match"
  description="Pakistan won the match by 5 wickets"
/>


Here:

* `title` is a prop.
* `description` is a prop.

The `NewsItem` component can receive these values.

---

## 3. How to Receive Props

In a class-based component, props can be accessed using:


this.props


Example:
class NewsItem extends Component {
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h3>{this.props.title}</h3>
        <p>{this.props.description}</p>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
]

### Roman Urdu

Agar parent component child ko data bhej raha hai:


<NewsItem title="Hello" />


To child component mein hum us data ko:


this.props.title


se access karenge.

---

# 4. Why Do We Use Props?

Props ka main purpose hai **components ke darmiyan data transfer karna**.

For example:


App.js
   ↓
News.js
   ↓
NewsItem.js


`App.js` data de sakta hai `News.js` ko.

`News.js` further data `NewsItem.js` ko props ke through de sakta hai.

---

# 5. State

State is used to store **data that can change inside a component**.

In a class-based component, state is written like this:

```jsx
state = {
  articles: [],
  loading: false
};
```

For example:

```jsx
class News extends Component {

  state = {
    articles: [],
    loading: false
  };

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h1>News</h1>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
```

---

# 6. Why Do We Use State?

State is useful when the data can change.

For example, initially:

```text
articles = []
```

After getting data from the API:

```text
articles = [news1, news2, news3, ...]
```

React updates the UI when the state changes.

### Roman Urdu

State ko aap component ki **memory** samajh sakte ho.

Pehle API se koi news nahi ayi:

```text
articles = []
```

API se news aa gayi:

```text
articles = [article1, article2, article3]
```

State update hone ke baad React screen ko bhi update kar deta hai.

---

# 7. Props vs State

| Props                            | State                              |
| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Data receive/send karne ke liye  | Data store karne ke liye           |
| Parent se child ko data deta hai | Component ke andar data rakhta hai |
| Usually read-only hota hai       | Change ho sakta hai                |
| `this.props` se access           | `this.state` se access             |
| Parent control karta hai         | Component manage karta hai         |

### Easy Example

```text
Props = Parcel 📦
State = Component ki memory 🧠
```

Props ke through component ko data milta hai.

State ke andar component apna changing data rakhta hai.

